Compile/run:
     - Compile using
            make MetroSim
     - run executable with
            ./MetroSim stationsFile passFile [commands]


Program Purpose:
---------------
     The purpose of this program is to create a train simulator 
     that allows the user to add and remove users to and from the 
     train


Acknowledgements: 
---------------
     I referred to Professor Kazerounian's presentations and looked up errors
     in Stack Overflow when a valgrind error confused me. I also referred to
     the CS15 website on interacting with files in C++ that was linked to
     on the main CS15 website

Files: 
------

main.cpp:
     Main file that reads in the given files and creates an instance
     of the MetroSim class

MetroSim.cpp:
     Implementation of MetroSim class.  Main functionality of running 
     the Metro Simulation, should be able to move trains and deal with 
     passengers going on/off the train.

MetroSim.h:
     Interface of MetroSim class.

PassengerQueue.cpp:
     Implementation of PassengerQueue class. Its primary purpose is to
     allow passengers to join and exit a queue (which will represent
     a car on the train)

PassengerQueue.h:
     Interface of PassengerQueue class

Passenger.cpp:
     Implementation of Passenger struct. These passengers are the
     elements that will make up PassengerQueue

Passenger.h:
     Interface of Passenger struct

stations.txt:
     an example file containing a list of stations.

test_commands.txt:
     sample list of commands that you could give to the simulator

the_metroSim:
     reference implementation for students to see how 



Data Structures:
--------------

     For creating PassengerQueue, I chose to use a linked list. Linked 
     lists are lists that are created by connecting together structs 
     called nodes, which contain an element and a pointer to the next 
     node in the list. Unlike array lists, linked lists do not have 
     indices and do not have a given capacity. However, like array lists, 
     they use heap memory to create their nodes. An advantage of linked lists
     is the fact that inserting or removing an element at the front will 
     be easier than with an array list since all of the subsequent elements
     do not need to be shifted over. A disadvantage is that since there are 
     no indices, it is a little harder to find a specific element in the 
     linked list based off of its position.
     
     My reasoning behind using a linked list for PassengerQueue was 
     that on a queue, you are only going to be either adding to the 
     back or removing from the front. Since retrieving elements in 
     the middle of the queue was not that important and since linked 
     lists are more effective than vectors at removing from the front, 
     I chose to use a linked list. Additionally, std::list already 
     contains functions that will remove from the front and add to 
     the back, unlike std::vector.

     For creating MetroSim, I decided to use an array list (std::vector)
     instead. An array list is another type of data structure that consists
     of an array that can grow as more elements are put into it. It is
     able to do this by using memory on the heap to create new arrays
     of different sizes. An advantage of array lists is that it is easier
     to find an element within the array since each element has an index.
     In MetroSim, it was important to be able to access an element at a 
     given index, such as when placing passengers onto the train or taking 
     them off. Linked lists are not equipped to handle finding elements 
     at an index, so using an array list via std::vector made the 
     implementation a lot easier. A disadvantage of array lists is that
     inserting elements at the front can take a long time since it needs
     to shift over all of the subsequent elements, but since the MetroSim
     class never needed to do this, this was not a problem.


Testing:
-------

     For this assignment, I started by unit testing the Passenger
     function print(). Since the only aspect of Passenger that I
     was allowed to edit was the print() function, I did not test
     to see if the different constructors worked. Since the print()
     function creates an output, I created my own Passenger and
     ran print() on it, visually checking the output to see that it
     had the desired formatting.

     Then, knowing that Passenger.cpp was correct, I moved on to
     PassengerQueue.cpp. This class had multiple functions I needed
     to test, so I started first with a simple one: size(). This was
     important to get right because many of the other unit tests
     rely on confirming the size of the queue is correct. I tested
     size() on an empty PassengerQueue, and it worked. After that,
     I tested the enqueue() function on an empty PassengerQueue
     and a PassengerQueue that already had a passenger enqueued 
     onto it. I created a separate unit test to confirm that the
     passengers were enqueued in the correct order and also that
     the PassengerQueue print() function works. Looking at the
     print() function's output, I could see that the passengers
     were placed in the right order and that print() outputs
     the correct string. I then tested the dequeue() function
     on a single element and on multiple elements.

     After creating PassengerQueue.cpp, I began working on MetroSim.cpp.
     In order to test this class, I moved most of the functions and
     variables from private to public. Because of this, once I moved
     them back to private, unit_tests would not compile unless I
     removed those lines. I left them commented out so you can see
     how I tested each function, even if these tests cannot run 
     anymore. I started by testing my constructor for MetroSim
     and making sure it started out with the correct values for
     certain variables such as the current station and number
     of passenger being zero. Then, I created two unit test that
     checked to see if the add_passenger() function worked when
     adding one passenger to an empty train and when adding
     multiple passengers. To make sure it was adding the passengers
     properly, I used to print() function and checked visually
     to see if the correct passengers were on the train. This
     essentially varified that the print() function worked, but
     I made a seperate unit tests to show that it worked when
     the train was empty, had one passenger, and had multiple
     passengers. Then, I checked the exit_train() function when
     one passenger needed to be removed and when multiple passengers
     should be removed. I also unit tested the add_station() function
     when a single station was being added to an empty vector for
     the station names and when multiple stations were being added.

     Up until this point, all of my functions had been working well,
     which I was pleasantly surprised by. Since my MetroSim seemed
     to be working so far, I moved on to creating and testing my
     main.cpp and the functions that could not be unit tested. I
     diff tested my MetroSim against the program that was giving
     using the provided station and commands file. It was at this
     point that I realized I had forgotten to make my program able
     to read in commands from a command file, which definitely does
     not bode well for diff testing. Once I solved that problem, I
     successfully diff tested it with good results, proving that
     my MetroSim functions worked for the most part. After that, I
     created a file that contained only a few stations and a
     command file that would move the train until it reached the
     first station again, which tests the looping stations edge
     case. My MetroSim passed that diff test, so I created a new
     command file that did not end in the command "m f" since 
     MetroSim should also terminate once the command file ends,
     no matter what the last command is. Diff testing this was
     not successful since my program would not terminate. Once
     I moved the if-statement that controlled that specific
     termination, it worked well.

     Then, I diff tested faulty input files, such as the station
     file and the command file not existing. Diff testing
     non-existant station files was successful, but when I ran
     valgrind on my program when inputing a non-existant command
     file, there were a lot of leaks. I figured out that it was
     because I opened the output file before opening the command
     file, so if my program terminated, then the output file
     would remain open, causing a leak. Reordering this fixed
     my problem, and my program worked smoothly after that.


Part that you found most difficult:
---------------------------------

     Even though we reviewed it in class, I still found using ifstreams
     and ofstreams to be very difficult. Reading from the station file
     was fine, but figuring out how to redirect specific outputs to
     an output file and bring in commands from a command file was a
     little harder. 

